[s0l0!-header][h1]Array of Function Pointers[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

An array of function pointers can replace a [b]switch[/b] or an [b]if [/b]statement for choosing an action, as in the following program: [code format="c" codeId="1756"]#include <stdio.h>

int add(int num1, int num2);
int subtract(int num1, int num2);
int multiply(int num1, int num2);
int divide(int num1, int num2);

int main() 
{
  int x, y, choice, result;
[b]  int (*op[4])(int, int);[/b]

  [b]op[0] = add;
  op[1] = subtract;
  op[2] = multiply;
  op[3] = divide;[/b]
  printf("Enter two integers: ");
  scanf("%d%d", &x, &y);
  printf("Enter 0 to add, 1 to subtract, 2 to multiply, or 3 to divide: ");
  scanf("%d", &choice);
  [b]result = op[choice](x, y);[/b]
  printf("%d", result);
    
  return 0;
}

int add(int x, int y) {
  return(x + y);
}

int subtract(int x, int y) {
  return(x - y);
}

int multiply(int x, int y) {
  return(x * y);
}

int divide(int x, int y) {
  if (y != 0)
    return (x / y);
  else
    return 0;
}[/code]
The statement [b]int (*op[4])(int, int);[/b] declares the array of function pointers. Each array element must have the same parameters and return type. In this case, the functions assigned to the array have two [b]int [/b]parameters and return an [b]int[/b]. The statement [b]result = op[choice](x, y);[/b] executes the appropriate function based on the user's choice. The previously entered integers are the arguments passed to the function.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

